00:40:14.278 --> 00:40:17.426
So, I'm not demonizing any of this.

1074
00:40:17.426 --> 00:40:20.702
But I do think it's important to at least take a moment

1075
00:40:20.702 --> 00:40:25.035
to pause on this notion of psychedelia for a moment,

1076
00:40:25.983 --> 00:40:29.406
because we're in a cultural moment right now,

1077
00:40:29.406 --> 00:40:34.073
where there is this convergence of that particular genus

1078
00:40:37.628 --> 00:40:40.881
of drug culture with the wellness movement.

1079
00:40:40.881 --> 00:40:43.506
Like those two circles are starting to overlap

1080
00:40:43.506 --> 00:40:45.604
and to create a Venn diagram.

1081
00:40:45.604 --> 00:40:48.220
And I think we need to appreciate

1082
00:40:48.220 --> 00:40:50.595
just how incredibly powerful

1083
00:40:50.595 --> 00:40:53.436
these mind altering substances are,

1084
00:40:53.436 --> 00:40:55.338
and that their use,

1085
00:40:55.338 --> 00:40:57.838
specifically their routine use

1086
00:40:59.540 --> 00:41:03.290
is not without ramifications or consequences.

1087
00:41:04.562 --> 00:41:05.395
Despite the fact

1088
00:41:05.395 --> 00:41:08.295
that there is some incredible research coming out,

1089
00:41:08.295 --> 00:41:09.988
that's been widely reported regarding

1090
00:41:09.988 --> 00:41:12.983
the therapeutic application of these substances,

1091
00:41:12.983 --> 00:41:14.152
which I don't dispute.

1092
00:41:14.152 --> 00:41:17.865
And I do celebrate when you see work coming out

1093
00:41:17.865 --> 00:41:20.532
of Johns Hopkins that psilocybin

1094
00:41:21.929 --> 00:41:25.453
can help ameliorate depression or PTSD.

1095
00:41:25.453 --> 00:41:26.286
Right.

1096
00:41:26.286 --> 00:41:28.496
And these other therapeutic applications

1097
00:41:28.496 --> 00:41:31.881
that are starting to emerge from the scientific community,

1098
00:41:31.881 --> 00:41:32.714
these are good things.

1099
00:41:32.714 --> 00:41:33.908
I'm celebrating that.

1100
00:41:33.908 --> 00:41:36.929
But I also think that there's a difference between that

1101
00:41:36.929 --> 00:41:38.262
and the kind of,

1102
00:41:39.114 --> 00:41:40.996
what happens is you see that

1103
00:41:40.996 --> 00:41:44.079
and then for some reason that creates

1104
00:41:44.975 --> 00:41:48.456
a permissive attitude around these substances

1105
00:41:48.456 --> 00:41:52.506
and it trickles down into what does become routine

1106
00:41:52.506 --> 00:41:56.055
or overly routine use with people, right?

1107
00:41:56.055 --> 00:42:01.023
So, if you look at Tim Ferriss talks about this all the time

1108
00:42:01.023 --> 00:42:02.231
on his podcast,

1109
00:42:02.231 --> 00:42:05.903
I think he's helped fund some of those studies coming out

1110
00:42:05.903 --> 00:42:06.749
of Johns Hopkins.

1111
00:42:06.749 --> 00:42:07.582
Oh, really.

1112
00:42:07.582 --> 00:42:09.328
If you read Michael Pollan's book.

1113
00:42:09.328 --> 00:42:10.627
That's a feedback loop.

1114
00:42:10.627 --> 00:42:12.647
Well, I think it's a personal interest to him.

1115
00:42:12.647 --> 00:42:13.480
Right.

1116
00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:15.847
I think that he's very emotionally engaged

1117
00:42:15.847 --> 00:42:17.680
with that whole thing.

1118
00:42:19.574 --> 00:42:20.858
And I think that's a good thing.

1119
00:42:20.858 --> 00:42:22.993
And he talks about all the time.

1120
00:42:22.993 --> 00:42:25.142
And then there's Michael Pollan's book,

1121
00:42:25.142 --> 00:42:26.031
"How to Change Your Mind",

1122
00:42:26.031 --> 00:42:29.593
and I think there's a lot that can be learned from.

1123
00:42:29.593 --> 00:42:32.748
He subjected himself to all of these different drugs

1124
00:42:32.748 --> 00:42:36.126
and reported on it and did a deep dive into the science.

1125
00:42:36.126 --> 00:42:36.959
So again,

1126
00:42:36.959 --> 00:42:41.546
I'm not raising this in any disparaging way whatsoever.

1127
00:42:41.546 --> 00:42:42.633
I celebrate all of this,

1128
00:42:42.633 --> 00:42:45.074
but I do think it should be said in my opinion,

1129
00:42:45.074 --> 00:42:50.074
that there is an epidemic right now of very unhappy people,

1130
00:42:50.577 --> 00:42:52.545
a lot of people who are in considerable pain.

1131
00:42:52.545 --> 00:42:53.735
And although some,

1132
00:42:53.735 --> 00:42:57.584
or perhaps even many of these people may be good candidates

1133
00:42:57.584 --> 00:42:59.493
for that kind of chemical therapy,

1134
00:42:59.493 --> 00:43:01.426
there are too many people out there

1135
00:43:01.426 --> 00:43:03.583
who are casually and irresponsibly looking

1136
00:43:03.583 --> 00:43:05.976
to these substances for answers.

1137
00:43:05.976 --> 00:43:07.888
For the answers that allude them,

1138
00:43:07.888 --> 00:43:10.448
in this effort to kind of shortcut

1139
00:43:10.448 --> 00:43:14.628
the path to greater self actualization or happiness,

1140
00:43:14.628 --> 00:43:17.778
or, at the end of that spectrum, enlightenment.

1141
00:43:17.778 --> 00:43:21.110
I've heard of people like actually going

1142
00:43:21.110 --> 00:43:24.246
and get ayahuasca treatment in Mexico

1143
00:43:24.246 --> 00:43:25.640
to stop taking opioids.

1144
00:43:25.640 --> 00:43:27.102
I know somebody who did that.

1145
00:43:27.102 --> 00:43:28.515
And I do too.

1146
00:43:28.515 --> 00:43:30.410
And I know people who have done that

1147
00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:31.687
and it's been successful for them.

1148
00:43:31.687 --> 00:43:32.520
Yeah.

1149
00:43:32.520 --> 00:43:34.317
So again, I'm not robbing those people

1150
00:43:34.317 --> 00:43:35.242
of those experiences.

1151
00:43:35.242 --> 00:43:37.128
I'm validating that.

1152
00:43:37.128 --> 00:43:38.408
But that's different

1153
00:43:38.408 --> 00:43:40.428
from I'm gonna do ayahuasca every weekend

1154
00:43:40.428 --> 00:43:42.323
or we're gonna take mushrooms all the time

1155
00:43:42.323 --> 00:43:44.876
because I'm unhappy and this is my solution.

1156
00:43:44.876 --> 00:43:46.768
It's more complicated than that.

1157
00:43:46.768 --> 00:43:48.867
And if ayahuasca was so effective,

1158
00:43:48.867 --> 00:43:50.034
then there'd be a lot

1159
00:43:50.034 --> 00:43:52.281
of enlightened people walking around right now.

1160
00:43:52.281 --> 00:43:55.026
Now, some of those people are better people

1161
00:43:55.026 --> 00:43:56.490
as a result of those experiences.

1162
00:43:56.490 --> 00:43:59.176
Again, I fully acknowledge that,

1163
00:43:59.176 --> 00:44:00.852
but I think there's also a lot of people

1164
00:44:00.852 --> 00:44:03.769
who are a little bit awash and lost

1165
00:44:04.748 --> 00:44:08.998
and return to those experiences looking for answers

1166
00:44:09.978 --> 00:44:11.936
that continue to elude them.

1167
00:44:11.936 --> 00:44:14.347
So there is no panacea here,

1168
00:44:14.347 --> 00:44:19.347
and I'm always suspect when something appears in the culture

1169
00:44:20.153 --> 00:44:23.733
as a shortcut to these things that,

1170
00:44:23.733 --> 00:44:25.130
all of us seek more of

1171
00:44:25.130 --> 00:44:27.777
and that there is a dearth of right now,

1172
00:44:27.777 --> 00:44:30.635
these feelings of engagement with ourselves

1173
00:44:30.635 --> 00:44:32.541
and the world and a sense of grounded,

1174
00:44:32.541 --> 00:44:35.864
belonging and community and love and compassion

1175
00:44:35.864 --> 00:44:37.649
and the like.

1176
00:44:37.649 --> 00:44:40.418
So I'm only sounding a little bit of,

1177
00:44:40.418 --> 00:44:42.258
not even really a cautionary tale,

1178
00:44:42.258 --> 00:44:45.070
but maybe just a pause button

1179
00:44:45.070 --> 00:44:47.560
so that we can reflect a little bit on this.

1180
00:44:47.560 --> 00:44:48.740
In Los Angeles right now,

1181
00:44:48.740 --> 00:44:51.650
the pot dispensaries are like Starbucks.

1182
00:44:51.650 --> 00:44:54.601
And there is this idea that like,

1183
00:44:54.601 --> 00:44:58.922
oh, well, THC is just part of your wellness routine

1184
00:44:58.922 --> 00:45:01.222
and like microdosing and all of these things.

1185
00:45:01.222 --> 00:45:02.988
And it's like,

1186
00:45:02.988 --> 00:45:06.262
I'm biased because I'm a product of 12 step

1187
00:45:06.262 --> 00:45:08.572
and I'm somebody who has addiction issues.

1188
00:45:08.572 --> 00:45:11.739
So, I have my own kind of set of ideas

1189
00:45:15.127 --> 00:45:15.960
around all this kind of stuff

1190
00:45:15.960 --> 00:45:17.855
that maybe make me a little bit more calcified

1191
00:45:17.855 --> 00:45:19.313
to the new ideas,

1192
00:45:19.313 --> 00:45:21.601
but I just think that that's dangerous.

1193
00:45:21.601 --> 00:45:22.758
Well, it's not just Los Angeles.

1194
00:45:22.758 --> 00:45:25.825
It just got the decriminalized nationwide

1195
00:45:25.825 --> 00:45:28.896
and it's in lots of places.

1196
00:45:28.896 --> 00:45:30.473
And I think the big fear

1197
00:45:30.473 --> 00:45:32.357
was young people were gonna be the ones

1198
00:45:32.357 --> 00:45:33.820
that ended up getting high all the time

1199
00:45:33.820 --> 00:45:35.399
and it impacts their brain development.

1200
00:45:35.399 --> 00:45:37.655
And what we've learned is it's the baby boomers

1201
00:45:37.655 --> 00:45:41.485
who go on QAnon and just get high and watch YouTube all day.

1202
00:45:41.485 --> 00:45:42.807
They're the ones who really,

1203
00:45:42.807 --> 00:45:44.352
we should have been watching out for this whole time.

1204
00:45:44.352 --> 00:45:46.716
Well, if they just did that, I'd be fine with it.

1205
00:45:46.716 --> 00:45:48.228
They just watch YouTube.

1206
00:45:48.228 --> 00:45:49.580
It's when it spills into the world

1207
00:45:49.580 --> 00:45:50.682
that it becomes problematic.

1208
00:45:50.682 --> 00:45:51.963
You know what?

1209
00:45:51.963 --> 00:45:54.411
I keep thinking of a couple of things.

1210
00:45:54.411 --> 00:45:59.109
One with in terms of hacking your way to something,

1211
00:45:59.109 --> 00:46:01.964
I think there is something inherently dangerous

1212
00:46:01.964 --> 00:46:03.689
in thinking that you can take a shortcut,

1213
00:46:03.689 --> 00:46:05.599
especially as something as,

1214
00:46:05.599 --> 00:46:07.316
look, you're 12 step,

1215
00:46:07.316 --> 00:46:10.030
but you're sober for years and years and years,

1216
00:46:10.030 --> 00:46:13.005
and maybe there's something to going slower

1217
00:46:13.005 --> 00:46:15.763
to get there and making it last longer.

1218
00:46:15.763 --> 00:46:16.596
I don't know,

1219
00:46:16.596 --> 00:46:17.622
I haven't looked at the studies,

1220
00:46:17.622 --> 00:46:20.578
but I wouldn't be surprised if there's a higher relapse

1221
00:46:20.578 --> 00:46:23.547
in people that do a shortcut versus that people

1222
00:46:23.547 --> 00:46:25.009
that are in the community

1223
00:46:25.009 --> 00:46:27.589
and doing the daily daily work that you do.

1224
00:46:27.589 --> 00:46:29.990
Are you saying that it's about the journey, Adam?

1225
00:46:29.990 --> 00:46:30.972
(laughing)

1226
00:46:30.972 --> 00:46:33.029
Well, speaking of journeys at the same time,

1227
00:46:33.029 --> 00:46:34.500
like the first time

1228
00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:36.193
I ever kind of had

1229
00:46:36.193 --> 00:46:40.681
that crack open love the world feeling, was tripping.

1230
00:46:40.681 --> 00:46:43.602
So to me psychedelics were an important part

1231
00:46:43.602 --> 00:46:45.521
of kind of crack in the open.

1232
00:46:45.521 --> 00:46:46.354
It was a portal.

1233
00:46:46.354 --> 00:46:47.187
Yeah, it's a portal.

1234
00:46:47.187 --> 00:46:49.252
Yeah, and like, look as a sober guy,

1235
00:46:49.252 --> 00:46:51.169
that's like a tripwire,

1236
00:46:52.310 --> 00:46:53.607
like that pushes my buttons.

1237
00:46:53.607 --> 00:46:58.524
'Cause I know sober people who have in sobriety volunteered

1238
00:46:59.827 --> 00:47:00.660
for psychedelic,

1239
00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:03.895
under supervision had psychedelic experiences.

1240
00:47:03.895 --> 00:47:05.527
And I think we've talked about this before,

1241
00:47:05.527 --> 00:47:09.349
and emerge from it saying that it was revelatory.

1242
00:47:09.349 --> 00:47:10.186
Right.

1243
00:47:10.186 --> 00:47:13.019
And so that's interesting to me.

1244
00:47:13.899 --> 00:47:15.540
That would be something that I would wanna check out.

1245
00:47:15.540 --> 00:47:17.773
Maybe, I don't know, I won't dismiss it,

1246
00:47:17.773 --> 00:47:20.690
but again, my instinct is when you,

1247
00:47:22.515 --> 00:47:24.353
that the answers that I'm seeking

1248
00:47:24.353 --> 00:47:25.848
are not gonna be found in a substance.

1249
00:47:25.848 --> 00:47:28.180
That's not to say that that might not open up

1250
00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:29.642
some portal for me,

1251
00:47:29.642 --> 00:47:33.916
and put right in front of me some blind spot that I have.

1252
00:47:33.916 --> 00:47:36.483
But when you tell somebody in recovery

1253
00:47:36.483 --> 00:47:38.951
that the answer to their problem lies

1254
00:47:38.951 --> 00:47:40.497
in a mind altering substance,

1255
00:47:40.497 --> 00:47:43.059
it's like, ding, ding, ding, ding, ding,

1256
00:47:43.059 --> 00:47:46.399
like everything lights up in your brain and that scares me.

1257
00:47:46.399 --> 00:47:47.232
Yeah.

1258
00:47:47.232 --> 00:47:48.065
Well, I get it.

1259
00:47:48.065 --> 00:47:49.499
I also actually don't even believe that.

1260
00:47:49.499 --> 00:47:52.506
I think what it did for me and I was very young at the time.

1261
00:47:52.506 --> 00:47:54.863
I was probably 20 at the time.

1262
00:47:54.863 --> 00:47:56.946
Is it showed me that love

1263
00:47:59.478 --> 00:48:02.880
can be unconditional and expansive.

1264
00:48:02.880 --> 00:48:07.562
And then I was dropped right back into my body (laughs)

1265
00:48:07.562 --> 00:48:08.529
that I didn't necessarily love,

1266
00:48:08.529 --> 00:48:10.530
or it didn't feel very expansive

1267
00:48:10.530 --> 00:48:14.731
and the way to actually get there in life,

1268
00:48:14.731 --> 00:48:18.430
in day-to-day life isn't to take that drug again and again.

1269
00:48:18.430 --> 00:48:19.599
You've got the lesson.

1270
00:48:19.599 --> 00:48:21.238
It's the lesson.
You've learned

1271
00:48:21.238 --> 00:48:22.071
what you needed to learn.

1272
00:48:22.071 --> 00:48:24.312
And then you have to keep moving in life

1273
00:48:24.312 --> 00:48:25.464
in a way that's productive.

1274
00:48:25.464 --> 00:48:28.040
And so he didn't have to do that

1275
00:48:28.040 --> 00:48:31.505
because he had too many resources at this disposal perhaps,

1276
00:48:31.505 --> 00:48:33.793
and he could do whatever he wanted any day.

1277
00:48:33.793 --> 00:48:36.384
There's danger to that level of freedom,

1278
00:48:36.384 --> 00:48:39.898
to not being tethered to having to do X, Y, or Z,

1279
00:48:39.898 --> 00:48:44.898
to get to pay bills or to accomplish this task or that task.

1280
00:48:44.978 --> 00:48:47.415
It's almost like freedom and happiness

1281
00:48:47.415 --> 00:48:50.348
were kind of what became the undertow.

1282
00:48:50.348 --> 00:48:52.566
And I wanted to touch on escapism

1283
00:48:52.566 --> 00:48:54.788
because I think that came up in a couple of the stories.

1284
00:48:54.788 --> 00:48:58.135
He was into escapism and I'm not against escapism

1285
00:48:58.135 --> 00:49:00.517
because if you look at the way the world is,

1286
00:49:00.517 --> 00:49:01.835
and the way people treat each other

1287
00:49:01.835 --> 00:49:03.734
and who would not want

1288
00:49:03.734 --> 00:49:05.479
to escape from that every once in awhile.

1289
00:49:05.479 --> 00:49:08.448
Like we escape in it in our different ways,

1290
00:49:08.448 --> 00:49:12.223
whether it's a trail run or a free dive session

1291
00:49:12.223 --> 00:49:14.849
or an open water swim, that's what it is.

1292
00:49:14.849 --> 00:49:16.373
That's to me what it is,

1293
00:49:16.373 --> 00:49:18.130
and I'm totally down with that.

1294
00:49:18.130 --> 00:49:22.047
I think you'd be crazy not to want to check out

1295
00:49:23.742 --> 00:49:25.758
for a little bit and try to reconnect

1296
00:49:25.758 --> 00:49:29.931
in something that's bigger than the that we see every day.

1297
00:49:29.931 --> 00:49:33.534
And I know a lot of your listeners are nodding in agreement

1298
00:49:33.534 --> 00:49:35.439
would feel the same exact way.

1299
00:49:35.439 --> 00:49:36.812
Escapism isn't the problem.

1300
00:49:36.812 --> 00:49:39.832
The problem is when escapism becomes everything.

1301
00:49:39.832 --> 00:49:41.212
Well, two things I agree.

1302
00:49:41.212 --> 00:49:44.529
And two other things that I take away from this.

1303
00:49:44.529 --> 00:49:48.946
One is the very significant emotional and mental toll

1304
00:49:50.391 --> 00:49:54.276
that the pandemic has on a lot of people, right?

1305
00:49:54.276 --> 00:49:57.879
This is a guy who could not be alone.

1306
00:49:57.879 --> 00:49:59.789
Like he needed to be around people.

1307
00:49:59.789 --> 00:50:01.394
And suddenly when that was removed

1308
00:50:01.394 --> 00:50:03.644
from the realm of possibilities,

1309
00:50:03.644 --> 00:50:07.283
I think that that impacted him disproportionately.

1310
00:50:07.283 --> 00:50:10.176
I think that's a big part of what led to the spiral,

1311
00:50:10.176 --> 00:50:11.507
and maybe being around people,

1312
00:50:11.507 --> 00:50:15.919
I don't know him and I'm, can't get inside his head,

1313
00:50:15.919 --> 00:50:18.833
but maybe him being around people all the time

1314
00:50:18.833 --> 00:50:20.775
with some kind of distraction

1315
00:50:20.775 --> 00:50:23.329
that kept whatever demons he had at bay,

1316
00:50:23.329 --> 00:50:27.158
being a social animal and missed a group of people

1317
00:50:27.158 --> 00:50:30.204
maybe he could be part of it, but also hide in it.

1318
00:50:30.204 --> 00:50:34.115
And when that's gone and you're forced to be with yourself,

1319
00:50:34.115 --> 00:50:36.345
the discomfort of that became unbearable.

1320
00:50:36.345 --> 00:50:37.861
And he started to look for different ways

1321
00:50:37.861 --> 00:50:39.278
to medicate that.

1322
00:50:40.141 --> 00:50:40.974
it's interesting,

1323
00:50:40.974 --> 00:50:42.712
he was interested in the communal mind, right?

1324
00:50:42.712 --> 00:50:45.384
He was interested in that, like the hive mind,

1325
00:50:45.384 --> 00:50:49.323
and maybe that was another escape, like you said to wait,

1326
00:50:49.323 --> 00:50:50.476
so he'd get out of his own mind.

1327
00:50:50.476 --> 00:50:51.309
Right.

1328
00:50:51.309 --> 00:50:53.512
The second thing is,

1329
00:50:53.512 --> 00:50:55.238
no matter how successful you get,

1330
00:50:55.238 --> 00:50:58.233
it's absolutely critical that you surround yourself

