2120
01:19:54.334 --> 01:19:57.064
like interviewing and asking it questions.

2121
01:19:57.064 --> 01:19:58.969
And in doing that,

2122
01:19:58.969 --> 01:20:01.857
there's you become a steward of the energies

2123
01:20:01.857 --> 01:20:03.761
of whatever the project is gonna be,

2124
01:20:03.761 --> 01:20:05.206
because that's what he sees a writer

2125
01:20:05.206 --> 01:20:06.930
as a steward of energies.

2126
01:20:06.930 --> 01:20:08.012
It's not just you.

2127
01:20:08.012 --> 01:20:12.012
Well, that's a very, big magic Elizabeth idea.

2128
01:20:14.772 --> 01:20:16.102
And he and Liz Gilbert,

2129
01:20:16.102 --> 01:20:17.746
I think there's a lot of overlap there.

2130
01:20:17.746 --> 01:20:20.915
He's saying, are you a writer?

2131
01:20:20.915 --> 01:20:23.754
If you write you're a writer and someday you'll become good.

2132
01:20:23.754 --> 01:20:25.088
You don't have to worry about becoming good.

2133
01:20:25.088 --> 01:20:27.092
That's not your job right now if you're a beginner.

2134
01:20:27.092 --> 01:20:30.399
He talks about the separation between flow and edit.

2135
01:20:30.399 --> 01:20:32.050
Flow, just let it come out,

2136
01:20:32.050 --> 01:20:34.451
edit, go back later and deal with,

2137
01:20:34.451 --> 01:20:35.774
which I've engaged.

2138
01:20:35.774 --> 01:20:37.311
That's definitely something I believe in,

2139
01:20:37.311 --> 01:20:40.408
I believe in certain amount of words per day

2140
01:20:40.408 --> 01:20:44.100
and getting that out and not necessarily rereading it

2141
01:20:44.100 --> 01:20:45.055
even the next day.

2142
01:20:45.055 --> 01:20:46.960
I try to get through a whole draft

2143
01:20:46.960 --> 01:20:49.208
before coming back and doing a full reread.

2144
01:20:49.208 --> 01:20:50.424
Sometimes that changes.

2145
01:20:50.424 --> 01:20:53.117
But that's what I try to do exactly for that reason.

2146
01:20:53.117 --> 01:20:55.414
There's a flow draft and there's an edit draft for me.

2147
01:20:55.414 --> 01:20:57.566
He talks about how it feels to him.

2148
01:20:57.566 --> 01:20:59.011
How is it feeling when you're writing?

2149
01:20:59.011 --> 01:20:59.844
Is it urgent?

2150
01:20:59.844 --> 01:21:00.866
Is it personal?

2151
01:21:00.866 --> 01:21:02.741
Is it spiritual?

2152
01:21:02.741 --> 01:21:03.785
I think that's interesting.

2153
01:21:03.785 --> 01:21:05.021
I don't really feel care

2154
01:21:05.021 --> 01:21:08.309
about how I feel when I'm riding that much,

2155
01:21:08.309 --> 01:21:11.243
but he's talking about the feel of the story itself.

2156
01:21:11.243 --> 01:21:13.775
Not how you feel about it,

2157
01:21:13.775 --> 01:21:16.940
but the feel of the actual product.

2158
01:21:16.940 --> 01:21:17.773
The feel of the product.

2159
01:21:17.773 --> 01:21:18.751
And that I got a lot out of.

2160
01:21:18.751 --> 01:21:19.991
I actually took something.

2161
01:21:19.991 --> 01:21:22.104
'Cause I never thought about the feeling of the book.

2162
01:21:22.104 --> 01:21:24.561
I've always thought about story points and characters,

2163
01:21:24.561 --> 01:21:26.792
but now I have a feeling that's like a theme,

2164
01:21:26.792 --> 01:21:30.622
and I'm only in the second draft right now.

2165
01:21:30.622 --> 01:21:33.322
I'm like two thirds of the way through a second draft.

2166
01:21:33.322 --> 01:21:35.222
And my goal is to have the second draft done

2167
01:21:35.222 --> 01:21:36.055
by the end of the year.

2168
01:21:36.055 --> 01:21:36.888
But then I,

2169
01:21:36.888 --> 01:21:38.199
this is gonna be a 10 draft process

2170
01:21:38.199 --> 01:21:39.581
to get this to any point

2171
01:21:39.581 --> 01:21:40.734
where I even give it to my wife to read.

2172
01:21:40.734 --> 01:21:42.688
It's gonna be a while.

2173
01:21:42.688 --> 01:21:46.588
So to have the feeling now I think is great.

2174
01:21:46.588 --> 01:21:48.413
So I got that directly out of it.

2175
01:21:48.413 --> 01:21:51.845
And the number one thing I got out of it is own it.

2176
01:21:51.845 --> 01:21:53.012
Own the piece.

2177
01:21:54.358 --> 01:21:55.376
Don't question.

2178
01:21:55.376 --> 01:21:56.261
If you're gonna write it,

2179
01:21:56.261 --> 01:21:58.572
don't keep questioning, are you good enough?

2180
01:21:58.572 --> 01:21:59.425
Are you doing it?

2181
01:21:59.425 --> 01:22:01.004
Is it worth doing?

2182
01:22:01.004 --> 01:22:02.897
Own the fact that it's worth doing

2183
01:22:02.897 --> 01:22:04.291
'cause you're doing it.

2184
01:22:04.291 --> 01:22:07.522
And I think that that level of confidence has been missing

2185
01:22:07.522 --> 01:22:10.957
and he just gives you permission to feel that way.

2186
01:22:10.957 --> 01:22:13.484
And so I think overall,

2187
01:22:13.484 --> 01:22:15.720
did I get a lot structurally out of it?

2188
01:22:15.720 --> 01:22:16.559
Not necessarily.

2189
01:22:16.559 --> 01:22:18.200
I don't write memoir.

2190
01:22:18.200 --> 01:22:19.783
He's a guy who was,

2191
01:22:20.741 --> 01:22:25.524
who basically writes about the Bible and writes memoir.

2192
01:22:25.524 --> 01:22:27.414
He writes some fiction stuff

2193
01:22:27.414 --> 01:22:29.299
he hasn't really shared necessarily.

2194
01:22:29.299 --> 01:22:33.352
Maybe he is a much more, he writes about anything he wants,

2195
01:22:33.352 --> 01:22:34.682
but the stuff that we know of him

2196
01:22:34.682 --> 01:22:36.357
is typically memoir related.

2197
01:22:36.357 --> 01:22:37.313
I don't do that.

2198
01:22:37.313 --> 01:22:39.918
Did he share anything about productivity

2199
01:22:39.918 --> 01:22:42.001
or sort of the resistant,

2200
01:22:43.873 --> 01:22:44.862
the creative resistance

2201
01:22:44.862 --> 01:22:46.958
and how to get over that kind of thing?

2202
01:22:46.958 --> 01:22:48.893
Because the reason I ask,

2203
01:22:48.893 --> 01:22:52.423
is he's one of the most prolific and productive,

2204
01:22:52.423 --> 01:22:54.320
creative people that I know,

2205
01:22:54.320 --> 01:22:58.804
He just doesn't ever seem to get gapped out in any way.

2206
01:22:58.804 --> 01:23:02.203
Like he's just constantly putting out amazing stuff.

2207
01:23:02.203 --> 01:23:04.110
And even his podcasts,

2208
01:23:04.110 --> 01:23:06.558
like the amount of intention and thought,

2209
01:23:06.558 --> 01:23:07.647
like most of them are monologues,

2210
01:23:07.647 --> 01:23:11.189
that could be characterized as sermons

2211
01:23:11.189 --> 01:23:13.382
and they feel very natural and casual,

2212
01:23:13.382 --> 01:23:16.734
but it's indisputable that he's thought deeply

2213
01:23:16.734 --> 01:23:19.118
about everything that he's saying.

2214
01:23:19.118 --> 01:23:22.285
And the naturalistic approach that he,

2215
01:23:23.877 --> 01:23:27.710
like it's so attitudinal because he does enter

2216
01:23:29.147 --> 01:23:31.120
into everything that he does

2217
01:23:31.120 --> 01:23:33.500
with a certain kind of enthusiasm and joy,

2218
01:23:33.500 --> 01:23:36.286
as opposed to the gnashing of the teeth.

2219
01:23:36.286 --> 01:23:38.013
There isn't seem to be a lot self doubt.

2220
01:23:38.013 --> 01:23:40.151
I know and I'm like, how does that work?

2221
01:23:40.151 --> 01:23:42.466
He doesn't have the self-doubt problem that we have.

2222
01:23:42.466 --> 01:23:44.578
And I don't know if it's, or I have,

2223
01:23:44.578 --> 01:23:45.411
I'm not,

2224
01:23:45.411 --> 01:23:46.255
I don't mean to say it.

2225
01:23:46.255 --> 01:23:47.247
But you got plenty of it?

2226
01:23:47.247 --> 01:23:48.080
Yeah.

2227
01:23:48.080 --> 01:23:49.070
And he doesn't seem to have it.

2228
01:23:49.070 --> 01:23:50.952
It seems like that could be a product of his faith.

2229
01:23:50.952 --> 01:23:53.834
The fact that he's got so much.

2230
01:23:53.834 --> 01:23:55.294
But I would suspect everybody

2231
01:23:55.294 --> 01:23:57.455
has these angsty moments, right?

2232
01:23:57.455 --> 01:23:58.756
But he seems to be,

2233
01:23:58.756 --> 01:24:01.220
I'm not standing over him watching him, right?

2234
01:24:01.220 --> 01:24:04.765
But we should be getting them up.

2235
01:24:04.765 --> 01:24:07.035
It feels like he's figured that out.

2236
01:24:07.035 --> 01:24:07.868
That would be,

2237
01:24:07.868 --> 01:24:09.341
it would be great to get them on.

2238
01:24:09.341 --> 01:24:10.174
And just to talk about creativity.

2239
01:24:10.174 --> 01:24:12.276
I've said this before, but he has,

2240
01:24:12.276 --> 01:24:13.976
I dunno if it's still up on his website,

2241
01:24:13.976 --> 01:24:15.871
'cause I listened to it quite a long time ago.

2242
01:24:15.871 --> 01:24:17.159
I suspect it is.

2243
01:24:17.159 --> 01:24:18.146
It's called,

2244
01:24:18.146 --> 01:24:20.559
I think it's called Something to Say,

2245
01:24:20.559 --> 01:24:25.559
and it's an audio lecture that is hours and hours and hours.

2246
01:24:26.166 --> 01:24:28.521
I think it's like eight hours or something like that.

2247
01:24:28.521 --> 01:24:31.810
It's like an unbelievably long lecture series of lectures

2248
01:24:31.810 --> 01:24:34.727
on basically how to create a speech

2249
01:24:37.421 --> 01:24:39.359
or a presentation or a keynote.

2250
01:24:39.359 --> 01:24:40.521
Like if you have something to say,

2251
01:24:40.521 --> 01:24:42.360
here's how you take an idea

2252
01:24:42.360 --> 01:24:45.527
and turn it into something worthwhile.

2253
01:24:46.813 --> 01:24:48.814
It's a masterclass on communication,

2254
01:24:48.814 --> 01:24:50.373
on clarity of thought,

2255
01:24:50.373 --> 01:24:52.611
on executing on an idea.

2256
01:24:52.611 --> 01:24:56.215
Of everything that I've consumed on the subject

2257
01:24:56.215 --> 01:24:58.202
of creativity and writing,

2258
01:24:58.202 --> 01:24:59.894
it was the most interesting,

2259
01:24:59.894 --> 01:25:03.144
thoughtful and helpful thing that I've ever listened to

2260
01:25:03.144 --> 01:25:04.723
and very different from anything else

2261
01:25:04.723 --> 01:25:05.950
that I'd ever consumed.

2262
01:25:05.950 --> 01:25:07.136
Well, this was not necessarily that.

2263
01:25:07.136 --> 01:25:09.419
So he didn't get too in the weeds on structure

2264
01:25:09.419 --> 01:25:10.944
and like helping you figure out.

2265
01:25:10.944 --> 01:25:11.777
Right.

2266
01:25:11.777 --> 01:25:12.610
This is a different thing.

2267
01:25:12.610 --> 01:25:13.883
This is a little bit more general

2268
01:25:13.883 --> 01:25:15.085
than I thought it might be.

2269
01:25:15.085 --> 01:25:18.376
But a couple of things that were really stuck out

2270
01:25:18.376 --> 01:25:20.281
is if you've written multiple things,

2271
01:25:20.281 --> 01:25:23.572
it's okay to go back to the drawing board

2272
01:25:23.572 --> 01:25:24.405
and write this one differently.

2273
01:25:24.405 --> 01:25:25.693
So to him, he's like,

2274
01:25:25.693 --> 01:25:26.976
how am I gonna write this one?

2275
01:25:26.976 --> 01:25:28.222
Am I gonna do maybe this one?

2276
01:25:28.222 --> 01:25:29.221
I'll do longhand.

2277
01:25:29.221 --> 01:25:31.737
Maybe this one I'll speak into a recorder

2278
01:25:31.737 --> 01:25:33.249
and have someone transcribe it.

2279
01:25:33.249 --> 01:25:34.988
He likes to keep it fresh.

2280
01:25:34.988 --> 01:25:37.497
For someone who's done a lot of writing,

2281
01:25:37.497 --> 01:25:38.568
and he's very prolific,

2282
01:25:38.568 --> 01:25:40.449
I think it was like nine books we went through, I forget.

2283
01:25:40.449 --> 01:25:42.282
He's very open minded.

2284
01:25:45.066 --> 01:25:48.264
He keeps that mind and that space as open as possible.

2285
01:25:48.264 --> 01:25:50.687
How do I write this one?

2286
01:25:50.687 --> 01:25:51.930
What does this one wanna be?

2287
01:25:51.930 --> 01:25:54.430
How do I align head and heart?

2288
01:25:55.972 --> 01:25:57.532
And like I said, I'll quote him here.

2289
01:25:57.532 --> 01:25:59.288
Here's the thing, own it, own it.

2290
01:25:59.288 --> 01:26:01.535
The thing you're trying to write, own it.

2291
01:26:01.535 --> 01:26:04.722
If it's a scifi novel, inventing an entirely new world,

2292
01:26:04.722 --> 01:26:08.332
make it as weird and wild and strange as you can.

2293
01:26:08.332 --> 01:26:10.357
Own it, own what you're trying to do.

2294
01:26:10.357 --> 01:26:11.312
And that's the key.

2295
01:26:11.312 --> 01:26:13.859
I think that writers can get into trouble,

2296
01:26:13.859 --> 01:26:16.262
trying to be too nice and he hates that.

2297
01:26:16.262 --> 01:26:18.894
And so that was a nice piece to hear from him.

2298
01:26:18.894 --> 01:26:23.685
Who is one of the nicer people (laughs)

2299
01:26:23.685 --> 01:26:24.539
for him to say that,

2300
01:26:24.539 --> 01:26:28.568
'cause he knows that that won't come across well on a page,

2301
01:26:28.568 --> 01:26:30.852
if something's too nice, if it doesn't have that edge,

2302
01:26:30.852 --> 01:26:33.838
'cause there's nothing for the reader to hang on.

2303
01:26:33.838 --> 01:26:35.421
And I think, to me,

2304
01:26:37.529 --> 01:26:39.719
he doesn't want people sanitizing their experience

2305
01:26:39.719 --> 01:26:41.392
or sanitizing their writing.

2306
01:26:41.392 --> 01:26:43.970
But for me, the things I got out of it is,

2307
01:26:43.970 --> 01:26:45.645
how do I write this one?

2308
01:26:45.645 --> 01:26:48.478
What is the feeling of this piece?

2309
01:26:50.113 --> 01:26:50.950
And own it.

2310
01:26:50.950 --> 01:26:52.425
Those are kind of the three main things I took.

2311
01:26:52.425 --> 01:26:53.646
It was very worthwhile.

2312
01:26:53.646 --> 01:26:55.577
And for somebody who's listening or watching,

2313
01:26:55.577 --> 01:26:57.973
who's not a writer who doesn't consider themselves

2314
01:26:57.973 --> 01:27:00.499
to be a writer, but perhaps as a creative spark,

2315
01:27:00.499 --> 01:27:03.832
of these ideas, what can be extrapolated

2316
01:27:04.774 --> 01:27:07.024
to anybody who's listening?

2317
01:27:08.508 --> 01:27:12.616
Like the lessons baked into this are specific to writing,

2318
01:27:12.616 --> 01:27:15.256
but they seem to me to be easily translatable

2319
01:27:15.256 --> 01:27:16.827
to other pursuits.

2320
01:27:16.827 --> 01:27:18.532
I think anytime that you have an idea

2321
01:27:18.532 --> 01:27:20.998
that you wanna pursue and you feel self doubt,

2322
01:27:20.998 --> 01:27:23.044
it's okay to own what you're trying to do

2323
01:27:23.044 --> 01:27:26.886
and not give yourself a third degree about it too often.

2324
01:27:26.886 --> 01:27:29.418
'Cause that can, in fact what you're trying to do,

2325
01:27:29.418 --> 01:27:31.730
it can, but insecurity can leak itself

2326
01:27:31.730 --> 01:27:33.620
into the work itself.

2327
01:27:33.620 --> 01:27:35.544
It doesn't always, but it can.

2328
01:27:35.544 --> 01:27:37.940
So I think owning it is good.

2329
01:27:37.940 --> 01:27:39.239
How do I wanna go about it?

2330
01:27:39.239 --> 01:27:40.799
And asking yourself those questions,

2331
01:27:40.799 --> 01:27:42.917
even if you're mid career in any job,

2332
01:27:42.917 --> 01:27:45.180
like say you're a lawyer and you're taking on a new case,

2333
01:27:45.180 --> 01:27:47.385
okay, how am I gonna research this case?

2334
01:27:47.385 --> 01:27:48.734
How am I gonna try this case?

2335
01:27:48.734 --> 01:27:49.981
Doesn't have to be the same way I've done,

2336
01:27:49.981 --> 01:27:51.430
even if they're all wins

2337
01:27:51.430 --> 01:27:53.542
What's the weirdest, craziest argument

2338
01:27:53.542 --> 01:27:54.584
I could come up with?

2339
01:27:54.584 --> 01:27:55.519
Right.

2340
01:27:55.519 --> 01:27:56.352
Yeah.

2341
01:27:56.352 --> 01:27:57.818
How do you attack each new project

2342
01:27:57.818 --> 01:27:59.828
from a fresh and different angle?

2343
01:27:59.828 --> 01:28:00.661
Yeah.

2344
01:28:00.661 --> 01:28:02.059
And then he comes with the,

2345
01:28:02.059 --> 01:28:03.946
don't be precious or attached to your writing.

2346
01:28:03.946 --> 01:28:05.281
I think that's very important,

2347
01:28:05.281 --> 01:28:08.568
that could be used for any idea in any job.

2348
01:28:08.568 --> 01:28:10.444
Don't be precious or attached to your ideas.

2349
01:28:10.444 --> 01:28:12.164
But he has something that's interesting.

2350
01:28:12.164 --> 01:28:13.754
He has a bits file,

2351
01:28:13.754 --> 01:28:16.962
stuff that doesn't fit into the,

2352
01:28:16.962 --> 01:28:19.483
cuts he's made that he wants to keep.

2353
01:28:19.483 --> 01:28:20.621
And then he puts it into a bits file.

2354
01:28:20.621 --> 01:28:23.445
And he's used that he gave a couple examples of using it.

2355
01:28:23.445 --> 01:28:24.340
That ends up in another book.

2356
01:28:24.340 --> 01:28:25.173
Yeah.

2357
01:28:25.173 --> 01:28:26.006
Right.

2358
01:28:26.006 --> 01:28:26.839
And I've never done that.

2359
01:28:26.839 --> 01:28:27.672
I always just move on completely

2360
01:28:27.672 --> 01:28:29.780
and start again from scratch.

2361
01:28:29.780 --> 01:28:30.631
So that's interesting.

2362
01:28:30.631 --> 01:28:32.604
It's been years since I listened to that,

2363
01:28:32.604 --> 01:28:33.766
something to say,

2364
01:28:33.766 --> 01:28:36.381
but I seem to recollect that one of the practices

2365
01:28:36.381 --> 01:28:39.926
that he talks about is just being more observant

2366
01:28:39.926 --> 01:28:40.981
in your daily life.

2367
01:28:40.981 --> 01:28:42.426
Because if you're paying attention,

2368
01:28:42.426 --> 01:28:44.602
there's weird stuff going on all over the place,

2369
01:28:44.602 --> 01:28:48.885
and making a habit of like writing one of those things down

2370
01:28:48.885 --> 01:28:51.345
every day, or like making a list of those things.

2371
01:28:51.345 --> 01:28:53.051
And the more that you do that,

2372
01:28:53.051 --> 01:28:56.435
the more engaged you are with the world and your environment

2373
01:28:56.435 --> 01:28:57.367
and the more you pay attention,

2374
01:28:57.367 --> 01:29:01.435
the more that that kind of fuels a creative spark.

2375
01:29:01.435 --> 01:29:02.268
I love it.

2376
01:29:02.268 --> 01:29:03.243
That just reminded me.

2377
01:29:03.243 --> 01:29:04.790
Maybe I just completely imagined that.

2378
01:29:04.790 --> 01:29:06.700
But I, pretty sure.

2379
